{
  "id": "KY-HB-564-2022",
  "scope": "state:ky",
  "title": "HB 564 (2022 Regular Session): AN ACT relating to elections and declaring an emergency.",
  "urls": [
    "https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/record/22RS/hb564.html",
    "https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/record/22RS/hb564/vote_history.pdf"
  ],
  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id)\n(section) Section 3(3) (new section of KRS ch. 117, in-person absentee)\n(quote) Any voter who votes an in-person absentee or federal provisional in-person absentee ballot shall provide proof of identification as defined in Section 22 of this Act or meet the requirements of KRS 117.228 or 117.229.\n(section) Section 22 (KRS 117.001(14), definition, reprinted)\n(quote) \"Proof of identification\" means a document that was issued by: (a) The United States or the Commonwealth of Kentucky, and the document contains: 1. The name of the individual to whom the document was issued; and 2. A photograph of the individual to whom the document was issued; (b) The United States Department of Defense, a branch of the uniformed services, the Merchant Marine, or the Kentucky National Guard, and the document contains:\n(section) Section 7 (KRS 117.085(2), old in-person absentee ID sentence)\n(quote) STRUCK: The voter who elects to vote in-person absentee shall provide proof of identification as defined in KRS 117.001 or meet the requirements of KRS 117.228 and 117.229.\n(section) Section 9 (KRS 117.086(3)(c), old in-person absentee paragraph)\n(quote) STRUCK: Any voter qualifying to vote who is unable to provide proof of identification as defined in KRS 117.001 may cast an in-person absentee ballot or federal provisional in-person absentee ballot in accordance with KRS 117.228 or 117.229.\n(b_no_id_remedy)\n(section) Section 3(3) (new section of KRS ch. 117); cross-reference only\n(quote) or meet the requirements of KRS 117.228 or 117.229.\n(c_mail_ballot_id)\n(section) Section 7 (KRS 117.085(1)(d))\n(quote) The online portal shall have the capacity to ensure the identity of the voter through proof of identification as required under KRS 117.227 or by means of KRS 117.228.\n(section) Section 7 (KRS 117.085, mail-in application paragraph)\n(quote) the voter shall provide a copy of his or her proof of identification, as defined in KRS 117.001, or the executed voter affirmation as described in KRS 117.228(1)(c).\n(section) Section 7 (KRS 117.085(1)(b))\n(quote) Acquiring a mail-in absentee ballot by means of the online portal shall require the voter to input personally identifiable information for verification.\n(d_proof_of_citizenship) not stated.\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) Section 1 (KRS 116.025(1))\n(quote) Every person who is a citizen of the United States, a resident of this state, and a resident of the precinct in which he or she offers to vote on or before the day preceding the closing of the registration books for any primary, general, or special election\n(other) Section 1 (KRS 116.025): who may vote, including that a person charged with but not convicted of a crime may vote and the rules for movers between precincts and counties (reprinted; I cannot see which subsection changed).\n(other) Section 2 (KRS 116.065): applications for registration, federal provisional ballots and absentee ballots are verified under penalty of perjury; adds a reference to Section 3.\n(other) Section 3 (new section of KRS ch. 117): creates 'no-excuse' in-person absentee voting on the Thursday, Friday or Saturday before an election (at least eight hours a day), and excused in-person absentee voting in the six business days before that Thursday, with listed excuses; keeps the ID rule (slot a).\n(other) Sections 4-6, 8-11 (KRS 117.025, 117.066, 117.079, 117.0851, 117.086, 117.087, 117.088): precinct lists, voting locations, absentee ballot return including secure drop boxes, challenges, voters with visual impairment; conforming changes to the new in-person absentee section.\n(other) Sections 12-17 (KRS 117.125, 117.135, 117.155, 117.165, 117.275, 117.383): voting-system approval, equipment custody, preparation and counting, State Board regulations.\n(other) Sections 18-21, 23-24 (KRS 118.115, 118.215, 118.365, 119.115, 118.015, 119.005): candidates, ballot certification, keys, definitions.\n(other) Section 22 (KRS 117.001): definitions, with 'election officer' added and the later items renumbered; the proof-of-ID definition is reprinted with its renumbering.\n(other) Section 25: emergency clause (the bill took effect on the Governor's approval).\n(other) version: orig_bill (introduced, BR 433): carries slots a, c, e. Cross-references read 'KRS 117.001' (not yet 'Section 22 of this Act'); same new in-person absentee section and the same portal paragraph.\n(other) version: 0087: this file is the printed Acts chapter text ('CHAPTER 87 (HB 564)'), not a floor amendment as the skeleton label says. It carries the same ID language in slots a and c as the other versions; the 'Section 22 of this Act' reference appears in it.\n(other) version: bill (furthest = enrolled, header 'HB 564/EN'): carries slots a, c, e; same ID language as 0087. Only the cross-reference to the definition changes from 'KRS 117.001' to 'Section 22 of this Act' between the introduced text and the later ones. I compared the ID passages, not the whole 3,300-line bill.\n(other) version: The House and Senate committee substitutes are now saved (see lines below); the saved enrolled text ('bill') matches SCS 1 on the ID passages and on every other passage the file comparison showed.\n(other) version: HCS1 (House committee substitute 1, 22 RS HB 564/HCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): carries slots a, c, e. Same in-person-absentee ID sentence in the new Section 3(3) and the same portal and mail-in ID language as the introduced bill; the cross-reference to the definition is already 'Section 22 of this Act'. Against the introduced text it adds a list item 'Any election officer tasked with election administration for the current election cycle' to the absentee-voting provisions, and adds wording that county board and counting-board members must not be 'of the same political affiliation'.\n(other) version: SCS1 (Senate committee substitute 1, 22 RS HB 564/SCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): carries slots a, c, e with the same ID language as HCS 1 (the ID sentence and portal paragraph are word for word the same). Against HCS 1 the only change of substance is in the risk-limiting audit pilot program, which gains a clause that the program sets the protocol for checking ballots against the tally results. No ID or citizenship change.\n(other) note: This bill adds no new ID rule. It creates a no-excuse in-person absentee period and MOVES the existing in-person absentee ID sentence out of KRS 117.085(2) and 117.086(3) (both bracketed as deleted; the closing bracket is not visible in the text, so the exact extent of deletion is an inference from where the same rule re-appears) into the new Section 3(3). The 117.228 affirmation and secondary-ID list are not reprinted here; slot b is cross-reference only. The skeleton labels this bill ADDED with no slots, which fits. Which subsection of KRS 116.025 this bill actually amends cannot be seen because underline marks are lost; subsection (1) with the citizen requirement is existing law and is also echoed in the 117.228 affirmation.\n(note) Off this topic: the bill sets no ID or citizenship-proof rule.",
